---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const warLists = assertion('the-two-war-lists-do-not-join')

const warListSpec = bar(
  warLists.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  warLists.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Allen County men in the World War', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="two-counts-of-one-war">
  <p>
      The same chapter of the same book that gives the Civil War gives the First World War.
      Chapter XLIV of the 1921 county history, “Allen County in the Wars”, runs fifty pages and
      ends in two rosters.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      The second half of that chapter had never been read here.
    </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={warLists} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="warlists"
    title="How many Allen County men were in the World War"
    caption="Three answers from one chapter. The first two are the American Legion's, reported by its Ohio commander; the third is a count of the names actually printed in the roster twenty pages later, and it is a floor — ninety-six further lines are visibly names the scan mangled."
    columns={['Count', 'Men']}
    rows={warLists.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="warlists" set:html={JSON.stringify(warListSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The roster belongs to Elmer McClain, an Allen County man who enlisted, did transport duty and
    kept a card system. He names twelve sources for it — ex-service men, the YMCA at the camps,
    the American Legion, the Federal Employment Bureau, the county recorder's office, the 1919
    Lima city directory, the township chairmen of the Victory Loan campaign, the Knights of
    Columbus, the Red Cross, the draft board, the historical society — and he says what it is
    worth: <em>the compiler knows that it is not in every respect either perfectly complete or
    perfectly accurate, but it is the most complete and accurate list in existence.</em> The book
    cannot spell him. He is Elmert in the paragraph that introduces the list and Elmer in the line
    above the first name.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ninety-five men are on the honour roll that follows, and the two lists do not join. Matched on
    surname and first initial, at most forty-seven of the ninety-five dead are missing from the
    roster of the served — and one of the missing is William Paul Gallagher. The service roster is
    alphabetical and the slot where he belongs runs Galberach, Gallant, Gallapool, Gallaspie,
    Gallont, Galloway: a continuous sequence with no Gallagher in it, which is what a name that
    was never entered looks like rather than one the scan lost. The American Legion post in Lima
    is the William Paul Gallagher Post No. 96, and it helped compile the honour roll.
  </p>
  <p>
    Against the county's other war the shape is inverted. One person in sixteen served, against
    one in ten in the Civil War; one in forty-four of them died, against one in eleven. Four times
    as many men went and a quarter as many died, in a war a fifth as long. And where the Civil War
    roster gave a cause of death for forty-nine men and let this site tally Corinth, Atlanta and
    Andersonville, this list gives ninety-five names and nothing else — no unit, no date, no
    place. The influenza that killed eight per cent of the cases at the state hospital that same
    autumn cannot be told apart in it from the Argonne.
  </p>
  <p>
    The county's war itself had no battle in it and no local beginning the sources will give. The
    Civil War opened on a named Sunday at the courthouse with a flag strung on a wire. This one
    opens with committees: a draft board serving without pay, a Council of Defense chaired by the
    man who owned the truck works, a woman's branch that changed hands to avoid overlapping the
    Red Cross, and a War Savings drive that beat its $1,297,000 quota by $70,000 — after which,
    the book says, <em>the people did not incline to make a sacrifice again.</em> Men were sworn
    in at Memorial Hall, from a radius many miles in every direction, and given lunch. What the
    county built for the front was a truck, and the book captions it the first one used in the
    war.
  </p>
</Article>
